In local testing today, I do see that you can hit errors when your initial `useEffect()` hook checks for an update, fetches, and reloads using the Updates API. This will happen if your app is configured to always check automatically for updates.

I am recommending two possible solutions:

- As mentioned in the comment above, you can modify your Expo config to only check automatically if there has been an error in launching the currently running app:

```json
{
  "expo": {
    ...
    "updates": {
      "checkAutomatically": "ON_ERROR_RECOVERY",
      ...
    }
  }
}
```


- If you want to have the automatic check, remove your code that looks like this

```js
  useEffect(() => {
    const updateCheck = async () => {
      const checkResult = await checkForUpdateAsync();
      if (checkResult.isAvailable) {
        await fetchUpdateAsync();
        await reloadAsync();
      }
    };
    updateCheck().catch((error) => console.warn(error));
  }, []);
```

and replace it with something like this, which listens for the events from the automatic update check.

```js
  Updates.useUpdateEvents((event: Updates.UpdateEvent) => {
    const updateLaunch = async () => {
      await fetchUpdateAsync();
      await reloadAsync();
    };
    if (event.type === Updates.UpdateEventType.UPDATE_AVAILABLE) {
      updateLaunch().catch((error) => console.warn(error));
    }
  });
```

I believe the above will resolve this issue for most. If you are still having the issue after following the recommendations above, please create a repo with instructions on how to reproduce the problem you are having, and we can reopen this issue, or create a new one if the problem is different from the above.

